## Conversation Summary

Date: 2026-09-17

### Objective

Split implementation responsibilities between two developers while preserving a single coherent domain model.

### Key decisions

- Assign one developer full ownership of the Member role and the other full ownership of the Librarian role.
- Design shared entities and interfaces together so that each shared rule has one implementation.
- Let the Member-role developer own member account, catalogue browsing, borrowing, and reservation workflows.
- Let the Librarian-role developer own member/book administration, item-condition handling, loan monitoring, fines, reservations administration, and librarian notifications.
- Treat authentication, repositories, and shared business entities as jointly designed components with an explicit primary owner and reviewer.

### Work completed

- Mapped each role to its UI, controller, services, workflows, and role-specific tests.
- Identified shared classes including `User`, `Book`, `BookCopy`, `Loan`, `Reservation`, `Fine`, `Notification`, authentication, and repositories.
- Defined integration workflows that both developers must test after their role features are complete.

### Open items / blockers

- Shared method signatures, database schema, and repository contracts were not yet frozen.

### Next steps

- Agree on shared contracts first, then implement role features without duplicating shared rules.
